<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kannur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cannanore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kannur, formerly Cannanore, is a city on the Malabar coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. A port of the spice trade, it was linked with the Kolathiri rulers and was the seat of the Arakkal family, the only Muslim royal house of Kerala. The Portuguese built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-angelo-fort">St Angelo Fort</a> there in 1505, later held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and the British.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kannur.jpg" alt="Kannur" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kidu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kannur_Skyline_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kannur lies on the coast of the Laccadive Sea, 280 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a> and 146 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangalore">Mangalore</a>, at an elevation of about one metre on a sandy shore. The city has an 8-kilometre seashore and a 3-kilometre beach at Payyambalam. Its climate is a very wet tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> one, with heavy rain from the southwest monsoon and an annual average of 3,438 millimetres. At the harbour of Mappila Bay stand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-angelo-fort">St Angelo Fort</a> on one side and the Arakkal palace on the other.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest traces of settlement are rock-cut caves, dolmens and megalithic burial sites of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> age. In the Sangam period (1st to 5th century CE) a powerful kingdom was based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezhimala">Ezhimala</a> nearby, and the ancient port of Naura named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> is identified with Kannur. Kerala Muslim tradition places some of the oldest mosques of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> in Kannur, Madayi and Dharmadom, linking them to the legend of the Cheraman Perumals.</p><p>Kannur was the headquarters of Kolathunadu, one of the four most important dynasties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Quilon</a>. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> the Arakkal kingdom held rights over the city and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshadweep">Laccadive Islands</a>. St Angelo Fort was built in 1505 by Dom Francisco de Almeida, the first Portuguese viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> captured it in 1663, it was sold to the Arakkal family in 1772, and the British seized it in 1790.</p><p>Kannur served as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s military headquarters on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s west coast until 1887. Its municipality, formed on 1 November 1866, was one of the first modern municipalities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>. Because the Company preferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> as its main stations, the town lost much of its earlier standing. Under British rule it was best known for Thalassery pepper.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kannur is the administrative headquarters of Kannur district and became a municipal corporation in 2015. At the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> its municipal area had 232,486 inhabitants, and the local language is Malayalam. It holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>'s only cantonment board and, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezhimala">Ezhimala</a>, the Indian Naval Academy. The district is the main centre of the ritual art Theyyam, and handloom weaving earned the city the name Manchester of Kerala. Kannur International Airport opened on 9 December 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kannur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Arakkal family were Mappila Muslim ministers and admirals of the Kolathiri rulers of Kolathunadu, based at <b>Cannanore</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/arakkal-kingdom">Arakkal kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Malabar posts were organised as a commandment governed from Fort Kochi, with factories at <b>Cannanore</b>, Cranganore and Quilon.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-malabar">Dutch Malabar</a></li><li><blockquote>Ezhimala, a hill on the coast north of <b>Kannur</b>, was the seat of chiefs praised in Sangam poetry, notably Nannan, a powerful and sometimes ruthless ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/ezhimala-kingdom">Ezhimala kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Kolathiri rulers claimed descent from the earlier Mushika dynasty of Ezhimala and controlled the coast around <b>Cannanore</b> and the pepper-growing hinterland.</blockquote><a href="/state/kolathunadu">Kolathunadu</a></li></ul></section>
